# Use autospec to keep mocks faithful to real signatures

## Description

A plain `Mock()` or `MagicMock()` accepts any attribute and any call signature, so tests keep passing after the real interface changes. Named fakes passed in as parameters come first (`pytest-test-doubles`); autospec is the fallback for a thin third-party seam the code cannot take as a parameter yet, such as an SDK client the code constructs itself. There, patch with `autospec=True` (or build the double with `create_autospec`), so the double rejects every call the real object would reject.

Patch the name where the code under test looks it up (`shop.signup.email_client`), not where the SDK defines it: the code holds its own reference, so patching the defining module changes nothing it calls.

## Bad Example

```python
from unittest.mock import patch


def test_sends_welcome_email() -> None:
    with patch("shop.signup.email_client.send") as send:  # plain Mock: accepts anything
        signup(email="alice@example.com")

    # The real signature became send(to=..., template=...) long ago;
    # this stale assertion still passes because a plain Mock accepts any call.
    send.assert_called_once_with("alice@example.com", "welcome")

    # A misspelled assertion method is swallowed too:
    # send.assert_caled_once()  # returns a Mock, never fails
```

## Good Example

{% if has_framework("pytest-mock") %}
```python
from pytest_mock import MockerFixture


def test_sends_welcome_email(mocker: MockerFixture) -> None:
    send = mocker.patch("shop.signup.email_client.send", autospec=True)

    signup(email="alice@example.com")

    # Fails with TypeError if the real send() signature drifts.
    send.assert_called_once_with(to="alice@example.com", template="welcome")
```

The project depends on pytest-mock, so the `mocker` fixture is available; `autospec=True` works the same with `unittest.mock.patch`.
{% else %}
```python
from unittest.mock import patch


def test_sends_welcome_email() -> None:
    with patch("shop.signup.email_client.send", autospec=True) as send:
        signup(email="alice@example.com")

    # Fails with TypeError if the real send() signature drifts.
    send.assert_called_once_with(to="alice@example.com", template="welcome")
```

`unittest.mock` ships with Python: use `patch(..., autospec=True)` as a context manager, and `create_autospec(EmailClient, instance=True)` when you build a standalone double to pass in rather than patch.
{% endif %}

## Notes

- `create_autospec(RealClass)` builds a signature-checked double without patching anything.
- `spec_set=True` also forbids setting attributes the real object lacks.
- autospec checks signatures, not return types: give the double return values shaped like the real ones.
- autospec on a very large class is slow; patch the narrowest name you can.
- Once the code can take the dependency as a parameter, replace the patch with a named fake (`pytest-test-doubles`).
- Whether autospec is the right double at all: choosing-a-test-double.md.
